especially after the Barbie movie, three of the top ten sellers or the Barbie dollhouse and others, where kids engaged in make-believe are learning how to tell narrative stories. And when they learn how to tell narrative stories and make it up themselves, they become better writers, better thinkers, and yes, they learn how to read better. So actually, the standard fair that's been around for a pretty long time are pretty good educational toys.
特别是在《芭比》电影之后，前十名畅销产品中有三个是芭比娃娃屋和其他玩具，孩子们在假装游戏中学习如何讲述叙事故事。当他们学会如何讲述叙事故事并自己编造时，他们会成为更好的作家、更好的思想家，是的，他们学会了更好地阅读。所以实际上，存在了很长时间的标准玩具是非常好的教育玩具。

47
00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:55.000
I guess people didn't know the term educational toys as an advertisement seller at that time, but to be honest, they're still pretty good from a psychological point of view. As long as they're fun, you know, I think you don't want kids to understand that what you're really doing is you're sneaking in some learning. Oh, well, you know, it's all learning at the end of the game, really. Our brain is working all the time. It just turns out that any time we get the chance to really be active learners, to be seeking information, to be curious and creative, that's when we learn the most. and I think as long as we appreciate that in ourselves and in our children, we're going to be better off because not everything is like IKEA furniture
我想人们当时并不知道“教育玩具”这个词作为广告卖点，但老实说，从心理学角度来看，它们仍然相当不错。只要它们有趣，你知道，我认为你不想让孩子们明白你真正做的是偷偷塞进一些学习内容。哦，好吧，你知道，游戏结束时一切都是学习，真的。我们的大脑一直在工作。事实证明，任何时候我们有机会真正成为主动的学习者，去寻求信息，去好奇和创造，那才是我们学得最多的时候。我认为只要我们在自己和孩子身上认识到这一点，我们就会变得更好，因为并不是所有东西都像宜家家具那样，

48
00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:27.000
where they tell you exactly where to put it. You know? Although it still doesn't come together. I was going to say to me it still doesn't come naturally, but my point is it doesn't, you know, these things, even in the Lego sets, they give you three things you can make from every one. It's not as much fun otherwise because you just make the one set and then you did it and it's done. Let's talk again about some of these electronic things that are out there, computer-based learning games.
他们告诉你确切地放在哪里。你知道吗？尽管它仍然组装不起来。我本来想说对我来说它仍然不自然，但我的意思是它不是，你知道，这些东西，即使在乐高套装中，他们也给你每套可以制作的三种东西。否则就没那么有趣了，因为你只是制作了一套，然后你就完成了。让我们再谈谈外面的一些电子产品，基于计算机的学习游戏。

49
00:30:27.000 --> 00:31:06.000
Many elementary schools are using games that are supposed to teach children math or reading as part of the curriculum. Does that count as playful learning or are they just like using a different tool? It's just not chalk on a blackboard. Most of it is just another tool, though there is some really good stuff out there. And if you can find it, there are places like common sense media and all that direct you to a lot of good places. But I would say, be careful. The real key is for kids to have choice and voice. And that kind of agency represents the active domain.
许多小学正在使用应该教孩子数学或阅读的游戏作为课程的一部分。这算作游戏化学习吗？还是它们只是像使用不同的工具？这只是不是黑板上的粉笔而已。大多数只是另一种工具，尽管外面确实有一些非常好的东西。如果你能找到它们，有一些地方像“常识媒体”等会引导你到很多好的地方。但我会说，要小心。真正的关键是让孩子有选择权和发言权。那种自主权代表了主动领域。

50
00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:44.000
A chance to be social, to have other people to do it with makes a big difference. And we've seen that even in educational apps when kids are working solo versus working together, they actually can learn more. So there is good stuff out there. Schools have found some of it. I don't know that everything has to be a game. You know, some things you just have to learn like the multiplication tables, and that's okay. On the other hand, I do want to be conscious that these more gamified platforms can sometimes be really fun for kids and kids do retain it.
有机会进行社交，有其他人一起做，这会有很大的不同。我们已经看到，即使在教育类应用程序中，当孩子们独自工作与一起工作时，他们实际上可以学到更多。所以外面有好东西。学校已经找到了一些。我不知道是否一切都必须是游戏。你知道，有些东西你只需要学习，比如乘法表，那没关系。另一方面，我确实想意识到这些游戏化平台有时对孩子来说真的很有趣，而且孩子们确实记住了。

51
00:31:44.000 --> 00:32:21.000
I know there are companies, again, everything that these companies have isn't great, whether it's Meadow or it's Roblox or whatever, that are building a lot of new platforms where the kids get to build the cities and the kids get to build the designs. That gives them choice. And I think those are even more powerful examples of how kids can learn. Because truthfully, you can't build a city if the math doesn't work. You know, you can't put a building on another building. So, you know, those are good ways to learn to. And let's talk about what you're working on now. I mentioned in the intro that you just got a $19 million grant.
我知道有一些公司，同样，这些公司所拥有的一切并不都是伟大的，无论是 Meta 还是 Roblox 等，它们正在构建许多新的平台，孩子们可以在那里建造城市，孩子们可以进行设计。这给了他们选择权。我认为这些是孩子们如何学习的更有力的例子。因为说实话，如果数学不成立，你就无法建造城市。你知道，你不能把一栋建筑放在另一栋建筑上。所以，你知道，这些也是很好的学习方式。让我们谈谈你现在正在做什么。我在介绍中提到你刚刚获得了 1900 万美元的资助。

52
00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:34.000
Yeah. What are you doing with that money? What are you hoping to learn? Yeah. I mean, it was even better. It was pretty close to $20 million. It was insane. But we're so proud of it and so excited.
是的。你打算用这笔钱做什么？你希望学到什么？是的。我的意思是，它甚至更好。它非常接近 2000 万美元。这太疯狂了。但我们为此感到非常自豪和兴奋。

53
00:32:34.000 --> 00:33:20.000
And what we're doing is we're working with funding from the Lego Foundation. We are working in four states. And I wish I could say it's just me, but I don't really wish it could say it's just me. Science is a team sport. And I've been, you know, so fortunate over the years to work with the top scientists in the country who have been writing about how to use the best learning techniques for children in schools for a long time. So we're going to be doing a longitudinal study from kindergarten through fourth grade. And we are going to be in Illinois, Texas, California, and Virginia in public school systems.
我们正在做的是，我们正在利用乐高基金会的资助进行工作。我们正在四个州开展工作。我希望我能说这只是我，但我并不真的希望说这只是我。科学是一项团队运动。多年来，我有幸与全国顶尖的科学家合作，他们长期以来一直在撰写关于如何在学校为儿童使用最佳学习技术的文章。所以我们将从幼儿园到四年级进行一项纵向研究。我们将进入伊利诺伊州、德克萨斯州、加利福尼亚州和弗吉尼亚州的公立学校系统。

54
00:33:20.000 --> 00:34:25.000
We've targeted some under-resourced schools. Certainly schools have very diverse populations. And with the team, we've designed how to actually operationalize the active and engaged, meaningful, socially interactive pillars that we use to define guided play and playful learning so that it can be used in a classroom environment. So for one, we think we have to have less whole group instruction and a little bit more of students working. in pairs. So moving not to totally no direct instruction, but less direct instruction in the schools. And when students can see how that works and they're talking to others as they're learning and they have choices and when it's more meaningful and it's more culturally relevant, or let me say, you know, expresses some of the community values is maybe a better way to put it, then I think
我们针对了一些资源不足的学校。当然，学校的学生群体非常多元。我们与团队一起设计了如何将我们用来定义引导式游戏和游戏化学习的“积极参与、有意义、社会互动”这三大支柱真正付诸实践，以便将其应用于课堂环境。首先，我们认为应该减少全班统一授课，增加学生结对学习的时间。也就是说，并不是完全取消直接教学，而是减少学校里的直接教学。当学生看到这种方式如何运作，在学习时能与他人交流，拥有选择权，并且学习内容更有意义、更具文化相关性——或者换句话说，更能体现社区价值观时，我认为

55
00:34:25.000 --> 00:35:25.000
they're going to learn better. We're using a coaching system in the schools where we will be working with coaches in each state who will be training coaches and teachers in the particular states. And then as we do that, we hope to leave these methods with the teachers and with the schools so that it can scale up and can be self-sustaining. And the other exciting part of it is, you know, years, I think since no child left behind and even before, we've known that the education system that we have in the United States, and I may say even globally earlier today I got off the phone with UNESCO, and I've also been working with the OECD in Europe, is that our education was fashioned for a prior era. It was fashioned for a time.
他们会学得更好。我们正在学校使用一种辅导系统，与各州的教练合作，由他们负责培训当地的教练和教师。通过这种方式，我们希望将这些方法留给教师和学校，从而实现规模化并保持自我持续发展。另一个令人兴奋的部分是，我认为自从《不让一个孩子掉队》法案出台以来，甚至在此之前，我们就已经意识到，我们现有的美国教育体系，甚至可以说全球的教育体系——今天早些时候我刚和联合国教科文组织通过电话，也一直在与欧洲的经合组织合作——都是为过去那个时代设计的。它是为那个时代量身打造的。

56
00:35:25.000 --> 00:36:19.000
when we had assembly lines and where we were talking about a manufacturing perspective. And that's where we get, whether it's our grade systems or whether it's our assessment systems or whether it's students sitting in rows listening passively to, you know, the brilliant person in the front of the room who is dispensing knowledge. And we need to change that model. We now live in an era of computers where kids have access to more information at their fingertips than they've ever had before. And knowledge is doubling and tripling at such a rate, even before AI. It was down to 12 months. All the information that we ever had in the world was doubling every 12 months.
那时我们有流水线，谈论的是制造业视角。这就是我们评分系统、评估系统，以及学生成排坐着被动听讲的由来，听着前面那位博学的人传授知识。我们需要改变这种模式。我们现在生活在计算机时代，孩子们触手可及的信息比以往任何时候都多。知识的增长速度正在成倍增加，甚至在人工智能出现之前，知识翻倍周期就已经缩短到了12个月。世界上所有的信息每12个月就会翻一番。

57
00:36:19.000 --> 00:37:01.000
Now there's AI. I mean, forget about it. It's probably every 10 seconds. So in that kind of a world, using an old-fashioned school system with an old-fashioned curriculum isn't going to get our kids to thrive. And the business world has moved on. But as Time magazine has said, if Rip Van Winkle came back today, the only thing that would look familiar is likely the school system. It's made teachers unhappy because they know it's not the way to teach. And we need to trust our teachers again.
现在有了人工智能，别提了，可能每10秒钟就会翻一番。因此，在这样的世界里，使用老式的学校系统和老式的课程体系无法让我们的孩子茁壮成长。商业世界已经向前发展了。正如《时代》杂志所说，如果“李伯大梦”今天醒来，唯一让他感到熟悉的东西可能就是学校系统。这让教师们感到不快乐，因为他们知道这不是正确的教学方式。我们需要再次信任我们的教师。

58
00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:56.000
We need to create a system where they are happy teaching and don't want to run out the door. We need a system where our kids are prepared for that workplace of tomorrow, to be good collaborators and communicators, to know their content but critically think, to be kids who have creative innovation, to have kids who also know how to take the risk and the confidence and the grit to move on and do things well. When we have a school system that creates those outcomes, which every parent wants for their child, then we're going to be able to mirror what the parents are saying, have a school system that's more modernized and future directed. and where teachers want to teach again. And that's what we hope to bring in this study. Well, that's a tall order, but it's important,
我们需要创造一个让他们乐于教学、不想逃离的系统。我们需要一个让孩子们为未来职场做好准备的系统，让他们成为优秀的合作者和沟通者，既掌握知识又能批判性思考，成为具有创造力和创新精神的孩子，同时也要让孩子们懂得如何承担风险，拥有自信和毅力去不断前进并把事情做好。当我们拥有一个能产生这些成果的学校系统时——这也是每位家长对孩子的期望——我们就能够呼应家长的诉求，拥有一个更现代化、面向未来的学校系统，让教师们重新渴望教学。这就是我们希望通过这项研究带来的成果。嗯，这任务艰巨，但很重要，

